{
  "FormatVersion": 3,
  "Id": "3f9a1d64-7c25-4b8e-9a13-6e5d84c07b21"/*BooleanOperation*/,
  "Description": "Boolean-combines MeshGeometry solids with a BSP kernel: union, difference and intersection.\n\nWith nothing connected to Operands the op is a pass-through: it hands Geometry on untouched, including all attributes and the part table, so it is safe to leave in a chain.\n\nEach operand's faces are inserted into a binary space partition of its own face planes and the two trees then clip each other, so a fragment survives only if it is on the requested side of the other solid. The cut surface of the result is made of the other operand's own faces - nothing has to be capped - which is why a closed input pair yields a closed output. Two faces that meet in one plane are decided by their facing instead of a point test: one copy survives where they face the same way, and both cancel where they face opposite. All parts of one input count as that one solid, so multi-part geometry such as separated letters or fracture chunks needs no merge step.\n\nCorner attributes (UVs, colours, normals) are carried through the cuts and interpolated at new corners. Faces the operation exposes - ones that came from an operand rather than from Geometry - are marked in the face attributes IsCut and Selection; a union exposes none and marks nothing.\n\nSolids are expected to be closed. The op warns when the result is not watertight: an open input has no inside to cut against, and two surfaces that pass within about a hundred-thousandth of the model size of each other can leave a few open edges. [BooleanOperationDiagnostics] runs the measured table of box pairs.\n\nAKA: CSG, subtract, cut out, merge, intersect",
  "InputUis": [
